//
//  Walkthrough:
//  The fix is one keyword: drop `static`. An instance field is per-object,
//  so two counters cannot see each other and a test cannot inherit the
//  previous one's leftovers.
//
//  That sounds too easy until you notice what the first test proves.
//  `LeakyCounter` has two *separate objects* and still shares state, because
//  `static` means "one per type", not "one per instance". A reader skimming
//  `new LeakyCounter()` sees a fresh object; the field says otherwise.
//
//  The discipline that follows: **build the fixture inside the test.** A
//  local is unshareable by construction. The common alternatives are both
//  worse:
//
//    · a field on a test class — shared across tests in most runners (xUnit
//      constructs a new instance per test, NUnit does not by default, and
//      relying on which is how a suite breaks when someone switches runner)
//    · a `[TearDown]` that resets the static — works only if nobody ever
//      forgets one, and not at all under parallel execution
//
//  The "would fail if the previous one leaked" and "order cannot matter"
//  tests are the ones worth copying into real suites. They are cheap, and
//  they turn "the suite is green" into "each test is green", which are not
//  the same claim.
//
//  Where this bites hardest in a web app: a `static` cache, a singleton
//  service holding request state (module 13/06), and an EF `DbContext` reused
//  across tests — its change tracker carries entities from the previous test
//  and relationship fixup then makes assertions pass for the wrong reason
//  (module 18/02). The next exercise handles the database case properly.
//  A test suite is only trustworthy if each test would pass ALONE. The
